From ca5e24b4a29e6bda78558b508598dc380472e240 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuk3s <44327449+nuk3s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 08:41:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kv-offload): treat an overrun of the total bound as a
 miss

Upstream base: the patch applies to local-inference-lab/vllm at
19f2c20ed4d64ae22bd009ceacadd85e2a81e354 after the 702 patches of
spark-ds41f-1120 (candidate-62, 0001 to 0829) and patches 9030 to 9041
of this series.

Rationale: the per-slot time of an IO thread includes the thread's own
Python work and its wait for the GIL, so busy Python threads in the same
process stretch the per-slot time on a healthy disk. If a load past the
total bound of 9041 counted as a fault, such a load would degrade the
whole tier on every rank until the engine restarted. With this patch, a
transfer that runs past the total bound while it still makes progress
(an overrun) is abandoned and reported failed with no fault. A load then
becomes a cache miss for its requests, a store is dropped, the IO thread
moves on to the next transfer, and the tier stays up. The worker reports
such loads in its metadata (overrun_load_events), and the scheduler does
not degrade the tier for them, while any other failed load still
degrades it. The progress check stays a fault and now also covers a
transfer that was already abandoned at the bound, so a read that hangs
after an overrun still fails the queue behind it. The worker logs one
warning for each overrun, with the bound, the slots done, and the
seconds, and a start line that names both bounds. disk_io_timeout_s and
disk_io_slot_budget_s must now be finite, because NaN and infinity
passed the old "> 0" check and turned a bound off. The submission times
of loads and stores, which no bound read any more, are removed.

Quality implications: the patch does not change outputs. An overrun
costs one prefill of the affected request, and the tier stays up for
other requests.

Tests: on the CPU path the offload suite passed 201 tests, with 2
CUDA-only modules skipped. In the new tests, a steady 0.1 s read misses
at the load's bound of 0.54 s with no fault, and the load queued behind
the slow load lands its bytes. A read that hangs after an overrun is a
fault at the progress timeout, and the queued load fails with no I/O. An
overrun with copies in flight on a late-copy stream drains the copies
before the release, and blocks refilled after the release keep the new
bytes. An overrun on one of three ranks is a miss, and the tier keeps
answering lookups. Further tests check that the overrun events
aggregate over the ranks, that the start line names both bounds, and
that NaN, infinity, 0, and -1 fail for both keys. The CUDA path of an
overrun was not tested, and no load overran during the hardware run.

Upstream status: the patch is proposed to spark-ds41f in
experiments/2026-11-22-kv-disk-tier and has not been submitted to
local-inference-lab/vllm.
